<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Order of Christ (Portugal)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1319–</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Military Order of Christ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Order of Christ was a Portuguese military religious order founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-rapontchombo">King Denis</a> in 1319, with the approval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a>, to take over the property of the suppressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>. Based at Tomar from 1357, it was governed in the fifteenth century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a>, whose voyages its revenues helped to fund, and its red cross flew on Portuguese sails. It survives as an order of merit.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/order-of-christ-portugal.jpg" alt="Order of Christ (Portugal)" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RS1974 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ordem_Honor%C3%ADfica_Portuguesa_-_Ordem_Militar_de_Cristo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
